# Maize: The 90-Kilo Bag Math — Getting More Than the National Average

Maize is the crop almost every Kenyan farm grows and almost no Kenyan farm plans. The national average sits around **8–12 bags per acre**, while the same seed on a planned acre commonly yields **25–40 bags**. The gap isn't seed quality — it's timing, nutrition, and storage. This post is the plan, with the numbers.

## Where maize grows well

- **Altitude:** 900–2,400 m for highland hybrids; 500–1,200 m for dryland OPVs
- **Rainfall:** 500–1,200 mm per season
- **Temperature:** 18–30 °C through the growing period
- **Soil:** well-drained loam at pH 5.5–7.0

The long rains (March–May) carry the main crop in most of central and western Kenya; the short rains (October–December) carry the second season where they are reliable — Meru and Kirinyaga sit in both envelopes.

## Pick the right seed for your rainfall

**Hybrids** (H6213, H629, WE 1101, SC Tembo-class) — bred for yield and uniformity, ~**3,500–4,500 KES per 10 kg bag**, plant **10 kg per acre** at **75 cm × 30 cm**. Choose these when your rains are dependable and you can afford the input. Expect **25–40 bags per acre** managed well.

**Open-pollinated varieties** (Katumani, DH04) — cheaper, re-plantable from your own seed, and the right choice in dryland zones where hybrid response doesn't pay. Lower ceiling, but far more resilient in a bad season.

The rule: match seed cost to rainfall reliability, not to the price of the bag.

## The timeline that matters

- **Week −2:** land prep; incorporate manure or crop residue. Plough early so the first rain finds you ready.
- **Week 0 (planting):** plant at first good rains, 1 seed per hill, 10 kg/acre. Band **50 kg DAP per acre** in the planting furrow — not on top.
- **Week 3:** first weeding — weeds compete hardest in the first six weeks.
- **Week 5 (knee-high):** top-dress **50 kg CAN per acre**, 10 cm from the row. This single step is the biggest yield lever on most farms.
- **Week 4–8:** scout for fall armyworm every 3–4 days — whorl damage is visible early, and early treatment saves the crop.
- **Week 14–18 (harvest):** when the grain tests at or below **13.5% moisture** (a fingernail stops denting the kernel). Harvesting early to "beat thieves" costs you more in weevil and aflatoxin risk than waiting costs in time.

Panda's season planner turns this into dated reminders on your phone — planting date, top-dress week, FAW scouting days — in English and Swahili.

## Pests and diseases that cost maize farmers money

1. **Fall armyworm** — the one that matters most. Scouting in the whorl stage catches it early; PCPB-registered larvicides applied at first sign, or hand-crushing in small plots. Late detection = 30–50% yield loss.
2. **Maize stalk borer** — entry holes in the stem; cut-and-destroy infected stalks after harvest, and scout from week 6.
3. **Maize lethal necrosis (MLN)** — chlorotic mottling and early death; no cure — rogue infected plants, use MLN-tolerant hybrids where available.
4. **Grey leaf spot** — rectangular grey lesions in humid mid-altitudes; rotate crops and keep rows open for airflow.
5. **Maize streak virus** — pale streaks, stunted cobs; control the leafhopper vector early and plant tolerant varieties.

Advisory note: Panda's treatment guidance is advisory — always use PCPB-registered products at label rates, and observe pre-harvest intervals. When in doubt, your local extension officer sees the same photo you do.

## Storage is where the money is made or lost

Post-harvest losses in Kenya run **20–30%** — mostly weevils and aflatoxin. The fix is cheap: dry to 13.5%, shell, and store in **hermetic bags** (PICS-style) instead of open gunny bags. Aflatoxin awareness matters too — reject mouldy grain at shelling, and never store damp maize.

Then the price game: maize prices bottom at harvest (June–July and January–February) and climb through the lean months (September–November, March–April). A farmer with hermetic storage can hold 8–12 weeks and capture **20–40% more per bag**. Panda's price checks for Meru and Kirinyaga markets will tell you the day to sell.

## The bottom line

A planned acre costs roughly **18,000–25,000 KES** all-in (seed, DAP, CAN, weeding, harvest) and returns **25–40 bags**. The same acre on habit returns 8–12 bags — the difference is a top-dress on time, a scouting walk, and dry storage. Maize isn't a low-margin crop; unplanned maize is.

